1
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:27,120
In 1929, the Grav Zeppelin flew around the world

2
00:00:27,121 --> 00:00:31,760
with 20 passengers, almost four years before the first solo

3
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:34,720
flight around the globe by an airplane.

4
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:37,500
For 12 and 1 1 days, the Graf's passengers

5
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:43,600
dined on gourmet meals, listened to music,

6
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,000
and looked out the window as they passed leisurely over regions

7
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,820
that no human being had ever seen from the air before.

8
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:56,240
This was airship travel at its peak, an evolutionary voyage

9
00:00:56,241 --> 00:00:59,540
that began with a basic balloon and a lot of hot air.

10
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:07,920
Man tried to fly for 5,000 years before he finally

11
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:10,560
hit on the idea of the balloon.

12
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:14,020
With no place to go but up in 1783,

13
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:17,700
the Montgolfier brothers created the first balloon that rose

14
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:20,940
under its own power, hot air.

15
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:24,000
They were constantly going around with sponges and water

16
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:25,900
to put out small fires.

17
00:01:25,901 --> 00:01:29,200
In December of that year, French chemist J.A. Charles

18
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:33,300
made the first manned flight in a balloon filled with hydrogen,

19
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:36,140
a much lighter source of lift.

20
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:39,320
It was about seven times lighter than air.

21
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:41,520
And it didn't take much to figure out

22
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,780
that that would help support flight,

23
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:45,320
and especially manned flight.

24
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:48,060
But hydrogen was considered flammable air

25
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:51,120
because it burns furiously, especially

26
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:54,460
when mixed with oxygen.

27
00:01:54,461 --> 00:01:56,900
The problem now facing the early aeronauts

28
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,300
was how to contain the lighter than air,

29
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:02,700
highly flammable hydrogen gas.

30
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:06,200
Fabric technology has had progressed by that time,

31
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,540
and they were starting to rubber coat balloons.

32
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:11,980
They found out once you flew, let's go somewhere.

33
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:13,280
We want to fly.

34
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:15,820
The balloon itself was a pretty limited vehicle.

35
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:17,580
After all, you couldn't steer it.

36
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:20,120
It simply floated to wherever the wind dug it.

37
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:21,980
A handful of aviators believe

38
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:23,820
that the best hope for controlled flight

39
00:02:23,821 --> 00:02:29,560
was a motorized steerable balloon, or dirigible.

40
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:32,100
Among them was a dashing young Brazilian balloonist

41
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:35,000
named Alberto Santos Dumont, who said

42
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:38,200
trying to propel a dirigible balloon through the air

43
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:42,500
is like pushing a candle through a brick wall.

44
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,180
In 1884, two members of the French Army's Balloon Corps

45
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:48,780
achieved an aeronautical first when

46
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,180
they navigated their electric-powered balloon

47
00:02:51,181 --> 00:02:55,920
back to its original starting point against the wind.

48
00:02:56,020 --> 00:02:57,980
The 23-minute flight was cut short

49
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:03,020
by the limited life of the engine's 500-pound batteries.

50
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:06,060
Engine weight haunted the airship pioneers.

51
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:08,100
The challenge now was to create a balloon that

52
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:11,700
was not only steerable, but streamlined and strong enough

53
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,940
to lift men and machines.

54
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:20,280
By 1897, Santos Dumont began work on his first airship.

55
00:03:20,281 --> 00:03:23,480
Santos made a great leap forward in engine technology

56
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:28,080
using a motorcycle-type motor for his airship

57
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:31,620
and did some innovative work in propeller design

58
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:34,220
that gave his ship greater efficiency.

59
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:37,200
It was a smaller engine, giving him more lift,

60
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:39,760
available lift for payload or for passengers.

61
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:42,640
In order to maintain the internal gas pressure

62
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:45,440
of the elongated and egg-shaped balloons,

63
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,980
aerial pioneers added ballonets, or airbags,

64
00:03:48,981 --> 00:03:51,920
that expand and contract to compensate for changes

65
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:55,420
in the pressure of the lifting gas.

66
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:58,920
This basic principle is still used in dirigibles or blimps

67
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:00,520
today.

68
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:03,220
Soon, the French added a rigid load-bearing keel

69
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:06,540
to the elongated balloon to help distribute the enormous weight

70
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,040
of the early electric and steam engines.

71
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:13,740
These semi-rigid airships were quite successful

72
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:16,840
and used as military lookouts.

73
00:04:16,841 --> 00:04:19,440
And then a man by the name of Count Zeppelin came along

74
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:20,740
and said, well, what would happen

75
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:23,340
if we took a whole bunch of balloons and put them together

76
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:25,340
and put a framework around them?

77
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:29,580
And from that was born the Zeppelin airship.

78
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:34,400
On July 2, 1900, a feisty Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin

79
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:39,260
staked his fortune and reputation on a crazy idea.

80
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,940
Three years before the Wright brothers' first flight,

81
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:45,260
the Count's first rigid airship took flight over Lake Constance

82
00:04:45,261 --> 00:04:47,020
in southern Germany.

83
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:52,460
It flew for 18 minutes and a distance of 3 and 1 half miles.

84
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:58,000
The LZ-1 for Luftschiff Zeppelin I was 416 feet long,

85
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:01,840
38 and 1 half feet in diameter, and had a lifting capacity

86
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:04,840
of more than 27,000 pounds.

87
00:05:04,940 --> 00:05:06,140
How did he do it?

88
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:08,480
Rather than fill one airbag with hydrogen

89
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:12,920
as his predecessors had, the count inflated 17 large gas

90
00:05:12,921 --> 00:05:16,420
and enclosed them in a rigid aerodynamic structure

91
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:19,720
of aluminum alloy, or deraumine.

92
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:22,100
The airship was powered by two engines

93
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:25,960
with the combined horsepower of a Volkswagen Beetle.

94
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:28,000
At the turn of the century, it was the invention

95
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,340
of the internal combustion engine

96
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:34,180
that helped make the Count's dream of a rigid airship a reality.

97
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:37,640
But they were expensive to build.

98
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:39,880
The German military said if Count Zeppelin

99
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:43,020
if Count Zeppelin can keep his airship in the air

100
00:05:43,021 --> 00:05:46,720
for 24 hours endurance, that they would be willing to purchase

101
00:05:46,721 --> 00:05:49,220
then his next airship.

102
00:05:49,221 --> 00:05:51,860
During one 24-hour endurance flight,

103
00:05:51,861 --> 00:05:54,460
Count von Zeppelin experienced engine trouble

104
00:05:54,461 --> 00:05:57,640
and put the airship down near the Daimler Engine Works

105
00:05:57,641 --> 00:05:59,200
and went to launch.

106
00:05:59,201 --> 00:06:02,240
It would prove fortuitous.

107
00:06:02,241 --> 00:06:06,600
In the meantime, a storm came and blew the airship away.

108
00:06:06,601 --> 00:06:09,200
Most unfortunate for the Count, because it didn't fulfill

109
00:06:09,201 --> 00:06:14,000
24-hour endurance requirement for government sponsorship.

110
00:06:14,001 --> 00:06:17,640
But what happened was a spontaneous flood of donations

111
00:06:17,641 --> 00:06:21,620
came from the German people to help pay for the next airship.

112
00:06:21,621 --> 00:06:24,320
He received about 7.5 million gold marks

113
00:06:24,321 --> 00:06:27,880
from the loss of his ship and from all the donations that

114
00:06:27,881 --> 00:06:30,620
came in to build the Zeppelin company

115
00:06:30,621 --> 00:06:32,360
and to build future airships.

116
00:06:32,361 --> 00:06:34,260
That was a technical marvel.

117
00:06:34,261 --> 00:06:37,020
As the Zeppelin began to work the bugs out

118
00:06:37,021 --> 00:06:39,720
and began to be used more or less routinely.

119
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:43,560
Just before World War I, there was a German Zeppelin

120
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:45,760
commercial service called the DeLag.

121
00:06:45,860 --> 00:06:47,460
The different Zeppelins of the DeLag

122
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:49,560
were making what we would call joyrides.

123
00:06:49,660 --> 00:06:51,860
You have to remember, aviation was totally new.

124
00:06:51,960 --> 00:06:55,980
And people really paid for their flights simply

125
00:06:56,080 --> 00:07:01,280
to have a bird's eye view of the city or town which they lived in.

126
00:07:01,380 --> 00:07:02,680
What's interesting to note, though,

127
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:06,120
is a number of the passengers were not Germans.

128
00:07:06,121 --> 00:07:09,060
The enthusiasm of taking a flight on an airship

129
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:13,220
had reached virtually every corner of the world.

130
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:16,400
By 1914, the Daylog airships carried more

131
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:21,340
than 34,000 passengers, over 100,000 miles.

132
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,240
Without a single accident, it was an economical success.

133
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:33,420
With the start of World War I, the Daylog commercial service

134
00:07:33,421 --> 00:07:38,420
came to an end, and Zeppelins were used for military purposes.

135
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:43,000
The Zeppelin was touted as the great secret weapon

136
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:46,060
that was going to revolutionize warfare.

137
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,440
The German rigid airships reigned supreme.

138
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:52,240
Capable of flying above 11,000 feet,

139
00:07:52,340 --> 00:07:55,500
they were well out of the range of ground-based anti-aircraft

140
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:59,680
guns, and not a single airship was lost to enemy fire.

141
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:02,540
The German military ordered 26 airships

142
00:08:02,541 --> 00:08:04,200
during the first year of the war,

143
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:07,420
and the Zeppelin company went into high gear.

144
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:09,780
They were producing a rigid airship basically every two

145
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:13,780
weeks off of one of the factories somewhere in Germany.

146
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:18,060
It takes a million rivets to produce one airship.

147
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:21,100
It also takes 700,000 skins, that

148
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:24,100
is the internal intestines of a cow,

149
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:27,100
to make all the gas bags for one airship.

150
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:29,940
It was a huge wartime effort.

151
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:32,240
Zeppelins were used by the German military

152
00:08:32,241 --> 00:08:35,280
for reconnaissance, as well as bombing raids

153
00:08:35,380 --> 00:08:36,880
over Great Britain.

154
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:39,640
They were above the clouds oftentimes.

155
00:08:39,740 --> 00:08:42,980
They did try using radio as a means of navigation.

156
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:46,480
And many a raid took place by Zeppelin,

157
00:08:46,580 --> 00:08:49,360
in which the commander would report that he raided this place

158
00:08:49,460 --> 00:08:51,300
or that place, and he was nowhere near it.

159
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:53,060
There was no bombing accuracy.

160
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:54,200
On the other hand, the airships

161
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:56,940
did a large amount of reconnaissance work,

162
00:08:57,040 --> 00:08:59,740
basically flying over the sea, watching out

163
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:02,240
for the British Navy.

164
00:09:02,241 --> 00:09:06,880
As the war escalated, the Germans built lighter and lighter airships,

165
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:10,840
capable of operating at an altitude of 20,000 feet.

166
00:09:10,841 --> 00:09:15,420
Frustrated by their lack of rigid airship technology,

167
00:09:15,421 --> 00:09:18,320
the British concentrated on building fighter planes,

168
00:09:18,321 --> 00:09:21,500
capable of climbing to 22,000 feet,

169
00:09:21,501 --> 00:09:25,660
and ammunition that could bring the high-flying Zeppelins down.

170
00:09:25,661 --> 00:09:29,660
The thing that really did the airships in during the First World War

171
00:09:29,661 --> 00:09:32,600
by the British of the so-called incendiary bullet.

172
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:35,540
A bullet like this hitting an airship filled with hydrogen

173
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:38,300
meant one bullet was all it took to bring an airship

174
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:40,340
down in flames.

175
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:42,680
It was not the great Zeppelin airships

176
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:46,280
that revolutionized warfare in World War I.

177
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:49,820
Instead, it turned out to be a small incendiary bullet.

178
00:09:53,420 --> 00:09:57,460
In March of 1917, at the age of 78,

179
00:09:57,461 --> 00:10:01,260
In 1978, Count von Zeppelin died.

180
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:04,260
Two Zeppelins dropped flowers as his coffin

181
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:05,560
was lowered into the earth.

182
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:15,120
At the end of World War I, the great German Zeppelin company

183
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:17,620
was forbidden from building any more airships

184
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:20,080
by the Treaty of Versailles.

185
00:10:20,180 --> 00:10:22,560
The remaining Zeppelin airships were to be divided up

186
00:10:22,660 --> 00:10:24,480
among the Allies.

187
00:10:24,481 --> 00:10:27,480
airship crews basically destroyed the majority

188
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:30,720
of the airships in their hangars before the treaty

189
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:32,620
went into effect.

190
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:35,560
The airships that the Germans did give over, for example,

191
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:38,600
to the French, they didn't give them the technology

192
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:41,700
as to how to operate them and how to maintain them.

193
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,700
That they kept unto themselves.

194
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:48,140
By 1921, the United States was interested in getting

195
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:49,800
its own airship.

196
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:51,440
They ordered a British-built version

197
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:53,740
of the high-flying German airships.

198
00:10:53,741 --> 00:10:57,980
And during the final stress test at sea, the R-38 snapped in two,

199
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:00,480
killing 44 sailors.

200
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:02,740
The airship's light frame buckled under the pressure

201
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:05,180
of high-speed turns.

202
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:08,480
Despite considerable apprehension from the Allies,

203
00:11:08,580 --> 00:11:11,220
the United States ordered an airship from the cash-strapped

204
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:14,360
Zeppelin Company as part of war reparations.

205
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:18,200
The ship was to be delivered in 1922.

206
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,360
In the meantime, the Americans decided

207
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:22,260
to build their own airship.

208
00:11:22,261 --> 00:11:24,300
They would call it the Shenandoah.

209
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:27,660
During the war, a German Zeppelin came down in France,

210
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:30,100
and the Allies had a chance to inspect it.

211
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:32,000
This was the L-49.

212
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:35,000
And as a result, they generated some drawings.

213
00:11:35,100 --> 00:11:37,600
The Navy acquired these drawings and basically

214
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:40,820
built their own Zeppelin in Philadelphia.

215
00:11:40,920 --> 00:11:44,380
The technology was very much World War I technology

216
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:45,820
on the Shenandoah.

217
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:48,620
There were some American refinements and improvements.

218
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:51,260
And of course, it flew with helium, not hydrogen.

219
00:11:51,261 --> 00:11:55,460
Helium is an inert gas first discovered in a few Texas oil

220
00:11:55,560 --> 00:11:56,500
wells.

221
00:11:56,600 --> 00:12:01,600
In 1922, the gas was in short supply and very expensive.

222
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:04,440
And even though helium provided less lift than hydrogen,

223
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:08,640
its primary advantage was that it does not burn.

224
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:11,440
The golden age of airships had begun.

225
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:14,380
The Shenandoah's first flight from Lakehurst, New Jersey,

226
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:18,620
marked the beginning of the era of American rigid airships.

227
00:12:18,621 --> 00:12:21,020
Americans were enchanted by the ship's flights

228
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:22,220
around the country.

229
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:25,260
She was hailed as the forerunner to a commercial passenger

230
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:26,420
airship.

231
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:29,260
However, her service was cut short.

232
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:32,860
In 1925, the Shenandoah got caught in a severe Midwest

233
00:12:32,960 --> 00:12:35,540
thunderstorm and broke in half.

234
00:12:35,640 --> 00:12:38,980
The 210-foot-long forward section carrying seven men

235
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:41,380
shot up to 10,000 feet.

236
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:43,320
Lieutenant Commander Charles Rosendahl

237
00:12:43,420 --> 00:12:45,480
figured he could fly what was left of the airship

238
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:48,620
under the same principles as a balloon.

239
00:12:48,621 --> 00:12:51,120
By dumping gasoline and valving off helium,

240
00:12:51,220 --> 00:12:54,020
he was able to fly what was left of the ship for almost two

241
00:12:54,120 --> 00:12:58,000
hours before the forward section of the Shenandoah crashed.

242
00:12:58,100 --> 00:13:02,100
While badly injured, Rosendahl and his six men survived.

243
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,840
14 others died.

244
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:14,080
In October of 1924, Americans were

245
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:17,440
able to get their first look at their second rigid airship,

246
00:13:17,441 --> 00:13:21,580
the German-built LZ-126.

247
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:23,480
This was the state-of-the-art airship

248
00:13:23,580 --> 00:13:25,480
design for the 1920s.

249
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:27,280
And the Germans believed it would be the last Zeppelin

250
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:28,380
they would ever build.

251
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:31,180
So they put extra care into its construction.

252
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:35,360
The LZ-126 was the vision of Dr. Hugo Eckner,

253
00:13:35,460 --> 00:13:38,560
a business-savvy and cautious Zeppelin pilot who

254
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:42,140
took over the company after Count von Zeppelin's death.

255
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:45,500
His favorite task was using the Zeppelin

256
00:13:45,501 --> 00:13:48,500
as a means of increasing friendship between nations.

257
00:13:48,501 --> 00:13:52,500
To him, the Zeppelin provided this means

258
00:13:52,501 --> 00:13:55,500
of international trade and commerce.

259
00:13:55,501 --> 00:13:59,500
Eichner was a very unique businessman,

260
00:13:59,501 --> 00:14:02,500
and that is very evident through the LZ-126,

261
00:14:02,501 --> 00:14:04,500
because when the ship was completed

262
00:14:04,501 --> 00:14:07,500
and due to make its flight to the United States,

263
00:14:07,501 --> 00:14:10,500
no insurance company was willing to take the risk.

264
00:14:10,501 --> 00:14:13,500
But he was so sure of the technology of that airship

265
00:14:13,501 --> 00:14:15,940
and that the flight would be successful,

266
00:14:15,941 --> 00:14:19,100
that he said, OK, we will ensure the flight ourselves.

267
00:14:19,101 --> 00:14:22,080
But he knew that the whole future of the Zeppelin cause

268
00:14:22,081 --> 00:14:23,540
was at stake.

269
00:14:23,541 --> 00:14:25,640
He had to deliver that airship.

270
00:14:25,641 --> 00:14:27,640
He took the risk, and it paid off.

271
00:14:27,641 --> 00:14:30,580
But he did land with very few gallons of fuel in the tank.

272
00:14:30,581 --> 00:14:32,220
Upon his arrival in the States,

273
00:14:32,221 --> 00:14:34,880
Eckner took a leisurely flight over Manhattan

274
00:14:34,881 --> 00:14:37,400
before delivering the ship to the Navy base

275
00:14:37,401 --> 00:14:39,920
in Lakehurst, New Jersey.

276
00:14:39,921 --> 00:14:42,820
The Navy immediately replaced the ship's hydrogen

277
00:14:42,821 --> 00:14:48,560
with non-flammable helium and renamed her Los Angeles.

278
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:51,320
You know, I see this thing in front of the hangar coming in.

279
00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:52,560
I didn't know what the heck I saw.

280
00:14:52,660 --> 00:14:54,360
It was so big.

281
00:14:54,460 --> 00:14:58,360
I mean, it looked like a mountain was floating in there.

282
00:14:58,460 --> 00:15:02,040
It was given to the Navy to use for experimental and training

283
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:03,380
purposes.

284
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:06,580
And it wasn't supposed to be used for war purposes

285
00:15:06,680 --> 00:15:07,720
or anything like that.

286
00:15:07,820 --> 00:15:10,580
The Los Angeles went on to have a long and distinguished

287
00:15:10,581 --> 00:15:12,380
as a Navy training ship.

288
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:15,620
She flew more than 4,000 hours, the most

289
00:15:15,720 --> 00:15:17,520
of any American rigid airship.

290
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:22,800
While the Germans were trying to reestablish a tradition

291
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:25,600
of building rigid airships, the British were having trouble

292
00:15:25,700 --> 00:15:27,160
with their designs.

293
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:30,700
Using downed zeppelins from World War I as models,

294
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:32,540
their technology was dated.

295
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:34,680
Nevertheless, in 1930, the British

296
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:37,380
rolled out one of the most unique airships to date.

297
00:15:37,381 --> 00:15:42,380
the R101 featured promenades and panoramic views.

298
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:44,660
At the last minute, an additional airbag

299
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:47,820
was needed in order to lift the lavish ship.

300
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:50,060
The British seemed to have a flair

301
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:54,300
for putting on too many things that were not necessary.

302
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:57,960
A ship's bell, for example, that probably weighed 20,

303
00:15:58,060 --> 00:16:01,800
40 pounds, things like that added up very quickly.

304
00:16:01,900 --> 00:16:04,500
54 passengers boarded the R101

305
00:16:04,501 --> 00:16:07,260
for her maiden voyage to India.

306
00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:10,100
150 miles from England, the airship crashed

307
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:13,300
in the French countryside, killing 48 people,

308
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:15,840
as well as the British rigid airship program.

309
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:20,880
The American airship program, on the other hand,

310
00:16:20,980 --> 00:16:22,620
was about to change.

311
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:26,160
Rich in helium, yet lacking the necessary technology,

312
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:29,160
the Americans went into business with the airship experienced

313
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:32,560
Germans forming the Goodyear Zeppelin Company.

314
00:16:32,561 --> 00:16:35,760
Here was an American firm who believed in building Zeppelins.

315
00:16:35,860 --> 00:16:38,960
P.W. Litchfield, who was the president of Goodyear,

316
00:16:39,060 --> 00:16:41,360
was very much an airship advocate,

317
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:45,100
and he negotiated to acquire the Zeppelin patents.

318
00:16:45,200 --> 00:16:47,200
They not only acquired the patent rights,

319
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:52,320
but they acquired 13 of the Zeppelin company's leading

320
00:16:52,420 --> 00:16:55,720
technologists, including Dr. Carl Arnstein,

321
00:16:55,820 --> 00:17:00,020
who had been one of the chief engineers in the Zeppelin company.

322
00:17:00,021 --> 00:17:01,620
He brought these other 12 over.

323
00:17:01,720 --> 00:17:04,960
They were known, incidentally, as the 12 disciples.

324
00:17:05,060 --> 00:17:08,020
They began work immediately on the USS Akron,

325
00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:11,760
the most innovative airship of her time.

326
00:17:11,860 --> 00:17:14,900
Construction started in the autumn of 1929,

327
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,340
and it was finished by the summer of 1931.

328
00:17:18,440 --> 00:17:20,700
She was a battleship of the sky.

329
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:22,740
The USS Akron was the first ship,

330
00:17:22,840 --> 00:17:26,380
and that was different in that it was designed from the outset

331
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:28,340
to be a flying aircraft carrier.

332
00:17:28,341 --> 00:17:31,340
inside the belly of the airship there was a hangar bay

333
00:17:31,440 --> 00:17:33,520
and it could carry up to five biplanes.

334
00:17:33,620 --> 00:17:35,620
They had several planes, but the most famous

335
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:41,280
was the Sparrowhawk, a little bit of a 20-foot wingspan fighter.

336
00:17:41,380 --> 00:17:43,560
It would come up, had a hook on the top,

337
00:17:43,660 --> 00:17:47,460
and it would hook onto a trapeze bar from a trapeze

338
00:17:47,560 --> 00:17:48,860
and lowered from the ship.

339
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:52,400
It would engage, and then the trapeze would raise the ship up

340
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:53,760
and store it in a hangar.

341
00:17:53,761 --> 00:17:56,660
in a hangar.

342
00:17:56,760 --> 00:17:58,740
And the idea behind this was that the planes

343
00:17:58,840 --> 00:18:01,560
could fly out and scout at a much greater distance

344
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:03,200
than the airship alone.

345
00:18:03,300 --> 00:18:06,080
The Akron had three reinforced keels

346
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:09,740
and eight engines capable of driving the ship at nearly 70

347
00:18:09,840 --> 00:18:12,080
miles per hour.

348
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:14,240
The engines were inside the ship,

349
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:16,780
and a long shaft came out there on the propellers.

350
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:21,780
And you just walk through a door into the engine compartment.

351
00:18:21,781 --> 00:18:26,720
In 1933, after logging more than 1,700 flight hours,

352
00:18:26,820 --> 00:18:31,260
the 200-ton airship flew into a storm off the New Jersey coast

353
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:33,820
and hit the water at full speed.

354
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:36,660
73 of the 76 men perished.

355
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:40,500
There were no life jackets on board.

356
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:42,860
Barely two weeks after the crash of the Akron,

357
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:44,900
her sister ship was completed.

358
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,580
The USS Macon was identical, except that she was 8,000

359
00:18:48,680 --> 00:18:50,980
pounds lighter and faster.

360
00:18:50,981 --> 00:18:53,380
the fact the new commanding officer found difficult

361
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:55,420
to deal with.

362
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:58,480
As one senior airship officer remarked to me,

363
00:18:58,580 --> 00:19:01,960
he was so careful it was downright embarrassing.

364
00:19:02,060 --> 00:19:04,120
And you can't, again, you can't really blame him.

365
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:06,420
I mean, he takes command of a ship.

366
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:09,960
The preceding ship was destroyed only two weeks before.

367
00:19:10,060 --> 00:19:12,500
Built to cruise 10,000 miles without refueling,

368
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:14,340
she's the pride of the Navy.

369
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:16,540
Using its fighter planes as lookouts,

370
00:19:16,640 --> 00:19:20,640
the Macon could patrol almost 129,000 square miles

371
00:19:20,641 --> 00:19:23,680
of ocean in a day.

372
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:27,840
In February of 1935, the Macon was lost in severe weather

373
00:19:27,940 --> 00:19:32,760
off the coast of California near Point Sur.

374
00:19:32,860 --> 00:19:35,120
This time, they had life jackets on board,

375
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:40,260
and all but two of the 83 men on board survived.

376
00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:44,000
When the Macon sank, the Navy was not interested enough

377
00:19:44,100 --> 00:19:46,460
in wanting to recover or do anything

378
00:19:46,560 --> 00:19:48,160
about bringing any of it back up.

379
00:19:48,161 --> 00:19:51,400
They figured they'd spent enough money down the drain

380
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:54,200
and had gotten nothing really from it except wrecks.

381
00:19:59,180 --> 00:20:03,880
On July 8, 1928, the LZ-127, or Graf Zeppelin,

382
00:20:03,980 --> 00:20:07,080
was christened by Count von Zeppelin's daughter.

383
00:20:07,180 --> 00:20:10,580
Nearly 10 stories high, the ship cleared the hangar door

384
00:20:10,680 --> 00:20:12,620
by only two feet.

385
00:20:12,720 --> 00:20:15,120
With the easing of sanctions imposed by the Versailles

386
00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:17,460
Treaty, the Germans were back in the Zeppelin business

387
00:20:17,461 --> 00:20:19,500
for themselves.

388
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:22,760
At the helm was Dr. Hugo Eckner, the Zeppelin's greatest

389
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:24,200
champion.

390
00:20:24,300 --> 00:20:26,460
This was the period of empire building.

391
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:31,080
The vision of Dr. Eckner was to see an intercontinental airship

392
00:20:31,180 --> 00:20:33,940
passenger service.

393
00:20:34,040 --> 00:20:38,520
At 775 feet, the Graf Zeppelin was two and a half football

394
00:20:38,620 --> 00:20:39,680
fields long.

395
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:42,020
And in early October, she set out

396
00:20:42,120 --> 00:20:45,760
on the first trans-oceanic voyage by an airship

397
00:20:45,860 --> 00:20:47,460
with passengers.

398
00:20:47,461 --> 00:20:54,360
It was designed to prove the airworthiness of rigid airships.

399
00:20:54,361 --> 00:20:55,110
That was Dr.

400
00:20:55,111 --> 00:20:59,920
Eckner's primary concern because you have to remember at this time, the early

401
00:20:59,921 --> 00:21:03,620
airlines, they were starting to make a name for themselves.

402
00:21:03,621 --> 00:21:10,820
And so airships, due to the dangerous nature of hydrogen, due to their size, tended to

403
00:21:10,821 --> 00:21:15,060
be still considered as a frail means of transportation.

404
00:21:15,061 --> 00:21:19,160
And so the 127 was really a very experimental airship.

405
00:21:19,260 --> 00:21:23,160
Equipped with two sets of gas cells, one for engine fuel

406
00:21:23,260 --> 00:21:27,280
and another for lifting gas, the Graf Zeppelin arrived safely

407
00:21:27,380 --> 00:21:29,040
in the United States.

408
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:32,440
The airship was met with great fanfare as thousands of people

409
00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:35,420
flocked to get a glimpse of her.

410
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:39,280
In bold pursuit of his dream of regular commercial transcontinental

411
00:21:39,380 --> 00:21:43,060
airship service, Hugo Eckner vowed to fly the Graf Zeppelin

412
00:21:43,061 --> 00:21:46,560
Not in a fictional 80 days, but in two weeks,

413
00:21:46,660 --> 00:21:50,660
the trip was projected to cost more than $250,000,

414
00:21:50,760 --> 00:21:54,000
money the Zeppelin company didn't have.

415
00:21:54,100 --> 00:21:56,300
As a friend of his once remarked to me,

416
00:21:56,400 --> 00:21:59,300
you know, it's remarkable that he kept that company going

417
00:21:59,400 --> 00:22:02,320
at the depths of the depression, particularly in Germany.

418
00:22:02,420 --> 00:22:05,620
So he knew how to get money and make money and the like.

419
00:22:05,720 --> 00:22:09,380
So too did newspaper magnate William Randolph Hearst,

420
00:22:09,381 --> 00:22:14,220
who immediately offered $150,000 to Eckner for the exclusive rights

421
00:22:14,320 --> 00:22:15,460
to cover the trip.

422
00:22:15,560 --> 00:22:19,120
Incredibly, the rest of the money came from stamp collectors.

423
00:22:19,220 --> 00:22:21,720
Notices would appear even in the New York Times

424
00:22:21,820 --> 00:22:24,400
or the papers in this country and everywhere else

425
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:26,760
that the ship was going to make this record flight.

426
00:22:26,860 --> 00:22:29,600
And now is your chance, everyone, to get some mail

427
00:22:29,700 --> 00:22:33,340
with a special stamp on it carried on the Graf Zeppelin.

428
00:22:33,341 --> 00:22:34,340
Zeppelin.

429
00:22:37,680 --> 00:22:39,180
The flight of the Graf Zeppelin

430
00:22:39,280 --> 00:22:43,280
provided classic newsreel images seen worldwide.

431
00:22:43,380 --> 00:22:45,380
To the magic of motion pictures,

432
00:22:45,480 --> 00:22:49,580
you will share with us the thrills and enjoyment of perhaps

433
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:52,100
the greatest aerial ventures in all history.

434
00:22:55,200 --> 00:22:59,600
We are now well out over the Atlantic.

435
00:22:59,700 --> 00:23:03,340
We are making 80 miles per hour.

436
00:23:03,341 --> 00:23:06,780
Inspecting the outer covering of the Zeppelin,

437
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:12,380
this chap makes certain to hold on to that rope.

438
00:23:12,480 --> 00:23:14,880
Several correspondents were on board,

439
00:23:14,980 --> 00:23:17,880
busy writing their stories for the Hearst newspapers.

440
00:23:22,100 --> 00:23:24,200
This is the dining room.

441
00:23:24,300 --> 00:23:29,240
Comfortable, even luxurious, worthy of any first class

442
00:23:29,340 --> 00:23:30,700
hotel on land.

443
00:23:33,340 --> 00:23:36,480
And now, as we sail, let's listen a while.

444
00:23:49,120 --> 00:23:50,620
England at last.

445
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:53,920
The Graf crossed the Atlantic in a record 42 hours

446
00:23:54,020 --> 00:23:57,400
and continued on her way to Germany.

447
00:23:57,500 --> 00:24:00,440
Berlin, where thousands in the streets

448
00:24:00,441 --> 00:24:07,440
below rejoice over the Zeppelin's triumph.

449
00:24:07,441 --> 00:24:15,440
The land of the rising sun in sight.

450
00:24:24,440 --> 00:24:28,440
And now for the unexplored skies of the Pacific.

451
00:24:28,441 --> 00:24:32,280
On the first non-stop flight ever made.

452
00:24:33,480 --> 00:24:37,940
Most of the time we fly in a strange uncanny universe

453
00:24:37,941 --> 00:24:41,280
with limitless seas of close-packed clouds,

454
00:24:41,900 --> 00:24:45,100
below a world in which time means little

455
00:24:45,101 --> 00:24:49,480
and the ordinary perceptions of distance even less.

456
00:24:58,440 --> 00:25:05,440
Our first glimpse of American territory.

457
00:25:12,620 --> 00:25:16,400
Directly over the Golden Gate, the sleek silver airship

458
00:25:16,401 --> 00:25:19,860
approaches San Francisco.

459
00:25:19,861 --> 00:25:25,160
The graph flies very, very low over the heart of the city.

460
00:25:25,161 --> 00:25:27,800
Occasionally, we are shaken up a bit,

461
00:25:27,801 --> 00:25:31,160
but we float safely over the mountains of the great southwest.

462
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:37,460
What an inspiring view of the grandeur that is America we are getting.

463
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:42,860
A voyage such as we have made is truly not only an education in geography,

464
00:25:43,720 --> 00:25:45,220
but a lesson in patriotism.

465
00:25:48,100 --> 00:25:53,140
Chicago is giving us as great a thrill as the Zeppelin is giving Chicago.

466
00:25:53,141 --> 00:25:59,620
In 12 flying days, we have traveled some 20,000 miles,

467
00:25:59,720 --> 00:26:04,920
spanned two continents, looked down upon 10 nations,

468
00:26:05,020 --> 00:26:08,920
conquered mountains and sea, and here we are,

469
00:26:09,020 --> 00:26:11,060
journey's end in sight.

470
00:26:15,320 --> 00:26:18,200
After successfully completing a trip around the world,

471
00:26:18,300 --> 00:26:21,200
Dr. Eckner was hailed in New York as a hero

472
00:26:21,201 --> 00:26:25,240
as a hero and considered one of the most famous men in the world.

473
00:26:25,340 --> 00:26:29,440
This flight has convinced me that the airship has

474
00:26:29,540 --> 00:26:32,380
passed the experimental stage.

475
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:33,840
Indeed, it had.

476
00:26:33,940 --> 00:26:37,720
The 7,000-mile nonstop flight from Germany to Japan

477
00:26:37,820 --> 00:26:42,060
was proof positive for many airship skeptics.

478
00:26:42,160 --> 00:26:43,960
She still had enough fuel on board

479
00:26:44,060 --> 00:26:45,960
to make another two days in the air.

480
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:49,060
So that means the rigid airship could have almost

481
00:26:49,061 --> 00:26:54,460
stayed up in the air nonstop for a week carrying passengers

482
00:26:54,560 --> 00:26:57,500
and safely.

483
00:26:57,600 --> 00:26:59,600
Soon, Eckner pioneered passenger

484
00:26:59,700 --> 00:27:02,100
service to South America.

485
00:27:02,200 --> 00:27:05,940
Only the well-to-do could afford a flight on an airship.

486
00:27:06,040 --> 00:27:08,120
The early flights, or these flights to North and South

487
00:27:08,220 --> 00:27:11,620
America cost approximately 1,000 Reismark, which

488
00:27:11,720 --> 00:27:16,820
was several years' income for a normal German worker

489
00:27:16,920 --> 00:27:17,920
at the time.

490
00:27:17,921 --> 00:27:20,660
It only took 2 and 1 1 days to North America

491
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:24,600
and 3 and 1 1 days to South America nonstop.

492
00:27:24,700 --> 00:27:27,860
Even the airplanes from Pan Am were not

493
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:30,340
able to make this type of a trip.

494
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:33,740
It was the fastest means of getting there.

495
00:27:33,840 --> 00:27:35,900
With still more skies to fly,

496
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:39,600
Eckner set off on a scientific expedition to the North Pole

497
00:27:39,700 --> 00:27:42,120
in July of 1931.

498
00:27:42,220 --> 00:27:43,980
Not only did they bring back information

499
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:46,940
about the Arctic's atmosphere, but the Graf Zeppelin

500
00:27:46,941 --> 00:27:50,340
proved she could fly in extreme conditions.

501
00:27:50,440 --> 00:27:53,520
Here again, cold weather didn't affect the operation

502
00:27:53,620 --> 00:27:54,820
of the airship at all.

503
00:27:54,920 --> 00:27:58,680
And so that was the big success and the big reasoning

504
00:27:58,780 --> 00:28:00,920
behind the 127.

505
00:28:01,020 --> 00:28:04,500
After that, Eckner could prove and safely say,

506
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:08,400
we can start an intercontinental passenger airship service

507
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:10,160
and make it work.

508
00:28:10,260 --> 00:28:12,840
While the Graf Zeppelin ruled the skies,

509
00:28:12,940 --> 00:28:16,940
Hitler and the Nazi party were navigating to rule Germany.

510
00:28:16,941 --> 00:28:19,800
If Eckner was to continue building his dream,

511
00:28:19,801 --> 00:28:22,580
he would soon have to make a deal with the devil.

512
00:28:26,640 --> 00:28:30,780
By 1933, Hitler and the Nazi party controlled Germany.

513
00:28:30,781 --> 00:28:33,920
They also controlled the fate of Dr. Eckner

514
00:28:33,921 --> 00:28:37,260
and his dream of intercontinental airship service.

515
00:28:37,261 --> 00:28:43,200
I hope I will be able in about six or seven weeks

516
00:28:43,201 --> 00:28:49,440
to take over the new airship to the United States of America.

517
00:28:49,441 --> 00:28:52,440
He was asked at one time to let Hitler

518
00:28:52,441 --> 00:28:55,780
speak at a political rally in the German Zeppelin company

519
00:28:55,781 --> 00:28:59,340
hangars at Friedrichshafen, and he refused.

520
00:28:59,341 --> 00:29:02,460
Well, you didn't do that and still remain very friendly

521
00:29:02,461 --> 00:29:04,520
with the Nazis.

522
00:29:04,521 --> 00:29:07,420
Eckner was an outspoken critic of the Nazi party

523
00:29:07,421 --> 00:29:10,220
and was only spared from the concentration camps

524
00:29:10,221 --> 00:29:12,720
of his popularity throughout the world.

525
00:29:12,820 --> 00:29:14,760
Because of the fame that he'd acquired

526
00:29:14,860 --> 00:29:16,700
in the trips around the world and the like,

527
00:29:16,800 --> 00:29:20,160
the Nazis wanted to make sure they could use Echner

528
00:29:20,260 --> 00:29:23,900
for promotional and publicity and propaganda purposes.

529
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,700
Dr. Echner did not like the Nazis.

530
00:29:26,800 --> 00:29:28,380
That is very obvious.

531
00:29:28,480 --> 00:29:30,940
But on the other hand, he saw the necessity

532
00:29:31,040 --> 00:29:32,740
to play with the devil, if you will.

533
00:29:32,840 --> 00:29:36,120
And that gave Dr. Echner the funds that he needed

534
00:29:36,220 --> 00:29:40,220
to begin his Intercontinental Airship service.

535
00:29:40,221 --> 00:29:42,020
The deal was this.

536
00:29:42,120 --> 00:29:44,320
Eckner needed money to build bigger hangars

537
00:29:44,420 --> 00:29:46,920
that could house an entire fleet of airships large enough

538
00:29:47,020 --> 00:29:49,460
to service the major cities of the world.

539
00:29:49,560 --> 00:29:51,860
Hitler agreed to finance Eckner's plans

540
00:29:51,960 --> 00:29:54,660
and formed an operating company which would control

541
00:29:54,760 --> 00:29:57,880
the airships out of Frankfurt.

542
00:29:57,980 --> 00:30:00,900
Early in 1936, one of Eckner's dream ships

543
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,880
became a reality with the unveiling of the Hindenburg,

544
00:30:03,980 --> 00:30:06,820
the epitome of everything the Germans had learned

545
00:30:06,920 --> 00:30:08,640
about rigid airships.

546
00:30:08,641 --> 00:30:11,980
She was the largest airship the world would ever see.

547
00:30:12,080 --> 00:30:15,440
I saw the Hindenburg, and I've never forgotten it.

548
00:30:15,540 --> 00:30:19,980
And I don't think anybody else ever did who did see it.

549
00:30:20,080 --> 00:30:24,620
At a length of 804 feet, she was 572 feet

550
00:30:24,720 --> 00:30:29,800
longer than a 747 jumbo jet and only 78 feet shorter

551
00:30:29,900 --> 00:30:31,000
than the Titanic.

552
00:30:31,100 --> 00:30:37,040
She weighed 130 tons and was as high as a 13-story building.

553
00:30:37,041 --> 00:30:41,280
The Hindenburg had a gas capacity of more than 7 million

554
00:30:41,380 --> 00:30:46,120
cubic feet, the volume of 300 average American homes.

555
00:30:46,220 --> 00:30:49,580
She was powered by four Daimler-Benz diesel engines

556
00:30:49,680 --> 00:30:54,160
capable of 1,320 horsepower each.

557
00:30:54,260 --> 00:30:57,400
Her aluminum alloy girders laid end to end would stretch

558
00:30:57,500 --> 00:31:00,360
for 13 miles.

559
00:31:00,460 --> 00:31:03,800
To Eckner's dismay, however, huge swastikas emblazoned

560
00:31:03,801 --> 00:31:07,480
emblazoned the ship's 105-foot tail fins.

561
00:31:07,580 --> 00:31:09,780
And it wasn't long before the Hindenburg

562
00:31:09,880 --> 00:31:14,820
and the beloved Graf Zeppelin went to work for the Nazi party.

563
00:31:14,920 --> 00:31:17,540
The Nazis needed the airships for propaganda purposes

564
00:31:17,640 --> 00:31:19,820
with the big swastikas on the tail.

565
00:31:19,920 --> 00:31:22,780
And Eckner had a fit about that.

566
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:24,960
Shortly after the Hindenburg was completed,

567
00:31:25,060 --> 00:31:29,100
the airships traveled to German cities of 100,000 or more,

568
00:31:29,200 --> 00:31:33,400
dropping leaflets promoting Hitler and Nazi party platforms.

569
00:31:33,401 --> 00:31:35,900
they continued to exploit the massive airship

570
00:31:35,901 --> 00:31:39,000
for political purposes at the Nazi Party's Congress

571
00:31:39,001 --> 00:31:44,580
in Nuremberg and the 1936 Olympic Games in Berlin.

572
00:31:44,581 --> 00:31:48,140
The Nazis replaced Eckner as the ship's cautious and conservative

573
00:31:48,141 --> 00:31:52,180
captain in favor of a pilot that was more politically acceptable,

574
00:31:52,181 --> 00:31:55,280
a move that didn't sit well with Eckner when the new pilot

575
00:31:55,281 --> 00:31:59,000
damaged the Hindenburg's tail fin.

576
00:31:59,001 --> 00:32:03,780
Eckner storms up to Lehmann, who was the captain in command

577
00:32:03,781 --> 00:32:06,720
of it at that time, and in a loud voice said,

578
00:32:06,721 --> 00:32:09,960
how could you take our brand new airship out just

579
00:32:09,961 --> 00:32:14,060
to please your lord and master Adolf Hitler?

580
00:32:14,061 --> 00:32:16,940
As a result, the press people were informed

581
00:32:16,941 --> 00:32:20,320
that from henceforth, Eckner's name and picture

582
00:32:20,321 --> 00:32:23,820
were not ever to be mentioned in the Nazi press again.

583
00:32:23,821 --> 00:32:27,900
So he was quite a brave fellow in that respect.

584
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:33,240
When the Hindenburg made its first trip to this country

585
00:32:33,340 --> 00:32:37,580
in 1936, Eckner was on board, but he had no title

586
00:32:37,680 --> 00:32:38,740
and no authority.

587
00:32:38,840 --> 00:32:40,740
They went around him, essentially.

588
00:32:40,840 --> 00:32:43,120
Of all the man-made birds that fly,

589
00:32:43,220 --> 00:32:45,280
this was the greatest.

590
00:32:45,380 --> 00:32:47,360
The Hindenburg was built above all

591
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:50,260
for intercontinental flight, and eventually,

592
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:51,860
that's what she did.

593
00:32:51,960 --> 00:32:55,220
In 1936, the airship made seven scheduled round trips

594
00:32:55,320 --> 00:32:59,000
to South America and 10 to North America.

595
00:32:59,001 --> 00:33:03,440
The Hindenburg was the largest man-made thing that ever flew.

596
00:33:03,540 --> 00:33:08,640
And it normally flew at altitudes of 1,000 feet or less.

597
00:33:08,740 --> 00:33:11,580
Now, when you had something three-city blockless long

598
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:15,380
flying directly overhead, making very little noise,

599
00:33:15,480 --> 00:33:17,880
it left a tremendous impression.

600
00:33:17,980 --> 00:33:20,020
Just as Eckner had imagined,

601
00:33:20,120 --> 00:33:22,460
the Hindenburg's passenger accommodations

602
00:33:22,560 --> 00:33:26,320
set a new standard in aircraft luxury.

603
00:33:26,321 --> 00:33:29,920
They had every comfort dreamable on board.

604
00:33:30,020 --> 00:33:34,260
Gourmet meals were cooked in an electric kitchen.

605
00:33:34,360 --> 00:33:36,060
The passengers had two bunks.

606
00:33:36,160 --> 00:33:37,900
There was two beds in a room.

607
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:40,640
Each room was equipped with hot and cold running water.

608
00:33:40,740 --> 00:33:42,200
There was also a shower.

609
00:33:42,300 --> 00:33:45,200
There was a writing room, which also had a piano on board.

610
00:33:45,300 --> 00:33:47,440
Of course, the piano was out of aluminum

611
00:33:47,540 --> 00:33:50,040
and didn't weigh more than about 40 pounds.

612
00:33:50,140 --> 00:33:53,620
The Hindenburg even had its own special smoking room,

613
00:33:53,720 --> 00:33:55,620
which was pressurized.

614
00:33:55,621 --> 00:33:59,800
to prevent any hydrogen gas from entering the room.

615
00:33:59,900 --> 00:34:02,560
In the smoking room was one lighter,

616
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:04,500
which was shared among all smokers.

617
00:34:04,600 --> 00:34:07,100
And you could only smoke with inside that room, which

618
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:10,060
made it, of course, a very unique airship.

619
00:34:10,160 --> 00:34:14,000
Passengers paid $400 one way to North America

620
00:34:14,100 --> 00:34:17,680
and 720 round trip.

621
00:34:17,780 --> 00:34:19,680
Filled with highly flammable hydrogen,

622
00:34:19,780 --> 00:34:22,980
the crew wore felt booties to prevent even the smallest

623
00:34:23,080 --> 00:34:25,620
spark from friction.

624
00:34:25,621 --> 00:34:27,420
But attention to the smallest detail

625
00:34:27,520 --> 00:34:30,260
would not be enough to prevent Hugo Eckner's dream

626
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:31,760
from going up in smoke.

627
00:34:37,660 --> 00:34:41,000
Only a handful of reporters were present in 1937

628
00:34:41,100 --> 00:34:43,980
when the Hindenburg made her final approach to the Navy

629
00:34:44,080 --> 00:34:46,140
base in Lakehurst, New Jersey.

630
00:34:46,240 --> 00:34:50,140
After all, Eckner's dream ship had made 17 transcontinental

631
00:34:50,240 --> 00:34:51,720
flights the year before.

632
00:34:51,721 --> 00:34:55,720
John Ionicone was working that day on the Navy ground crew.

633
00:34:55,820 --> 00:34:58,720
He was one of 198 sailors on station

634
00:34:58,820 --> 00:35:02,660
and the only military man still alive who saw what happened.

635
00:35:02,760 --> 00:35:05,660
JOHN Ionicone, John Ionicone, was working that day

636
00:35:05,760 --> 00:35:07,160
on the Navy ground crew.

637
00:35:07,260 --> 00:35:11,680
He was one of 198 sailors on station and the only military

638
00:35:11,780 --> 00:35:14,580
man still alive who saw what happened.

639
00:35:14,680 --> 00:35:18,120
JOHN Ionicone, John Ionicone, was supposed to come in at 6

640
00:35:18,220 --> 00:35:20,580
o'clock that morning, but it was 12 hours late.

641
00:35:20,581 --> 00:35:21,980
started to clear up.

642
00:35:21,981 --> 00:35:23,680
In the meantime, the ship got all wet.

643
00:35:23,681 --> 00:35:25,720
They were coming in for a landing,

644
00:35:25,721 --> 00:35:29,460
and we could see the whole ship from where we were all the time.

645
00:35:29,461 --> 00:35:31,620
She dropped the lines, and the ground crew

646
00:35:31,621 --> 00:35:35,020
had a hold of the two nose lines.

647
00:35:35,021 --> 00:35:36,100
And it was a routine landing.

648
00:35:36,101 --> 00:35:37,340
Everything was routine.

649
00:35:37,341 --> 00:35:39,500
And then all of a sudden, right there in front of the tail,

650
00:35:39,501 --> 00:35:43,100
I saw a big red glow inside the ship.

651
00:35:43,101 --> 00:35:46,380
And then it broke through the cover, and it just burned.

652
00:35:46,381 --> 00:35:47,480
And it's crashing.

653
00:35:47,481 --> 00:35:48,181
It's crashing.

654
00:35:48,182 --> 00:35:48,980
Terrible.

655
00:35:48,981 --> 00:35:49,681
Oh, my.

656
00:35:49,682 --> 00:35:50,880
Get out of the way, please.

657
00:35:50,881 --> 00:35:52,420
It's burning, bursting into flames,

658
00:35:52,421 --> 00:35:54,160
and it's falling on the mooring fast,

659
00:35:54,161 --> 00:35:55,320
and all the folks between us.

660
00:35:55,321 --> 00:35:56,120
This is terrible.

661
00:35:56,121 --> 00:35:58,680
This is one of the worst catastrophes in the world.

662
00:35:58,681 --> 00:36:04,800
Oh, it's like 20, oh, 400 or 500 feet into the sky.

663
00:36:04,801 --> 00:36:06,800
It's a terrific crash, lady, still,

664
00:36:06,801 --> 00:36:09,660
but the smoke is in flames now, and the framers

665
00:36:09,661 --> 00:36:13,240
crashing to the ground, not quite to the mooring fast.

666
00:36:13,241 --> 00:36:17,440
Oh, the humanity, and all the...

667
00:36:17,441 --> 00:36:21,060
I saw one man jump out of the nose.

668
00:36:21,061 --> 00:36:23,720
And when it was up there about 400 feet, and he got killed.

669
00:36:26,740 --> 00:36:28,680
We saw one man walk out.

670
00:36:28,681 --> 00:36:32,600
And he was burnt from the top of his head to his heels.

671
00:36:32,601 --> 00:36:33,800
All he had on was his shoes.

672
00:36:41,860 --> 00:36:44,620
When we got to the passenger compartment,

673
00:36:44,621 --> 00:36:46,340
I mean, there was people still in it.

674
00:36:46,341 --> 00:36:48,000
We told them to get out, and we started looking around

675
00:36:48,100 --> 00:36:49,180
for some more.

676
00:36:49,280 --> 00:36:52,680
They wasn't even scratched.

677
00:36:52,780 --> 00:36:56,180
The Hindenburg, which took four years to build,

678
00:36:56,280 --> 00:36:59,080
was destroyed in approximately 37 seconds.

679
00:37:05,400 --> 00:37:08,000
What was but a moment ago, the queen of the air

680
00:37:08,100 --> 00:37:13,440
is now a shell of consuming flame, a tragic memory.

681
00:37:13,540 --> 00:37:16,340
On the night of May 6, 1937, the Green

682
00:37:16,341 --> 00:37:21,840
The greatest airship ever built lay in a smoldering, twisted heap.

683
00:37:21,841 --> 00:37:25,140
I stood watch on it that night, tried to keep the looters out of it.

684
00:37:25,141 --> 00:37:25,980
But you couldn't do it.

685
00:37:25,981 --> 00:37:27,780
I mean, everybody's come in and they want a little piece of this,

686
00:37:27,781 --> 00:37:29,980
a little piece of that.

687
00:37:29,981 --> 00:37:32,880
Miraculously, 62 people survived.

688
00:37:32,881 --> 00:37:36,520
The Americans and Germans held hearings to investigate the cause

689
00:37:36,521 --> 00:37:39,860
of the crash that killed 35.

690
00:37:39,861 --> 00:37:42,160
The Germans said it was sabotaged, most of them,

691
00:37:42,161 --> 00:37:46,500
because they could not bring themselves to a frame of mind

692
00:37:46,600 --> 00:37:51,200
to admit that there could be any deficiency in the airship.

693
00:37:51,300 --> 00:37:52,900
Eckner was not the same way.

694
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:55,700
I talked to him at some length about what did he,

695
00:37:55,800 --> 00:37:57,500
the greatest airship man of all time,

696
00:37:57,600 --> 00:38:00,220
think about what destroyed his airship.

697
00:38:00,320 --> 00:38:02,520
And he was convinced it made a sharp turn

698
00:38:02,620 --> 00:38:04,820
before it approached the mast.

699
00:38:04,920 --> 00:38:07,720
He thinks that there was a broken wire that slashed open

700
00:38:07,820 --> 00:38:09,520
a gas cell.

701
00:38:09,521 --> 00:38:15,100
that hydrogen escaped, caught and collected up inside the ship and started to leak out

702
00:38:15,101 --> 00:38:22,820
of it, and that the static electricity in the air was enough to set the thing off.

703
00:38:22,821 --> 00:38:30,940
I believe the Hindenburg crash as such marked the end of the airship as an effective passenger

704
00:38:30,941 --> 00:38:34,180
vehicle for intercontinental travel.

705
00:38:34,181 --> 00:38:37,720
You have to remember airplanes had reached that magic point

706
00:38:37,721 --> 00:38:41,620
of technologically surpassing or at least achieving

707
00:38:41,621 --> 00:38:44,020
the same thing even in a shorter period of time,

708
00:38:44,021 --> 00:38:46,100
to be true or for, than the airships.

709
00:38:46,101 --> 00:38:49,800
So the days of the airship, the ridges were numbered.

710
00:38:49,801 --> 00:38:52,700
The Hindenburg is no more.

711
00:38:52,701 --> 00:38:55,100
But this catastrophe will not dim the vision

712
00:38:55,101 --> 00:38:57,800
nor stop the work of the undaunted pioneers.

713
00:38:57,801 --> 00:39:08,620
The Germans completed another ship, virtually a sister ship of the Hindenburg, and they

714
00:39:08,621 --> 00:39:14,680
had made something in the order of 40 flights in 1938 and 39.

715
00:39:14,681 --> 00:39:19,820
But it could never enter commercial service because the United States would not sell it

716
00:39:19,821 --> 00:39:20,820
helium.

717
00:39:20,821 --> 00:39:26,220
And nobody was going to ride a hydrogen-filled passenger

718
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:27,020
airship again.

719
00:39:35,160 --> 00:39:36,860
With the start of World War II,

720
00:39:36,960 --> 00:39:40,040
the Nazis deflated and dismantled the remaining rigid

721
00:39:40,140 --> 00:39:41,300
airships.

722
00:39:41,400 --> 00:39:43,480
The once peaceful giants of the sky

723
00:39:43,580 --> 00:39:46,880
were scrapped and melted down for fighter planes.

724
00:39:46,980 --> 00:39:50,180
The US Navy still was interested in airships,

725
00:39:50,181 --> 00:39:53,380
but knew that politically they could never build

726
00:39:53,480 --> 00:39:55,420
a really large one again.

727
00:39:55,520 --> 00:39:58,520
So they went instead to more blimps,

728
00:39:58,620 --> 00:40:01,820
small, non-rigid airships.

729
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:05,720
A fraction the size of the rigid airships.

730
00:40:05,820 --> 00:40:08,940
Their job was patrolling for U-boats,

731
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:12,740
and their squadrons were based all the way from Canada,

732
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:15,940
down along the East Coast, the Gulf Coast,

733
00:40:16,040 --> 00:40:17,940
in the Caribbean, down to Brazil,

734
00:40:17,941 --> 00:40:21,180
and we flew them to Africa, to the Mediterranean.

735
00:40:21,280 --> 00:40:25,280
They escorted something like 70,000 ships,

736
00:40:25,380 --> 00:40:30,020
with only two lost to an enemy submarine attack.

737
00:40:30,120 --> 00:40:33,220
After the war, Hugo Eckner served as a consultant

738
00:40:33,320 --> 00:40:34,760
to the Goodyear Company.

739
00:40:34,860 --> 00:40:37,660
Prior to his death in 1954, he said,

740
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:40,460
the Zeppelin has given way to the airplane.

741
00:40:40,560 --> 00:40:42,960
A good thing has been replaced by a better.

742
00:40:47,940 --> 00:40:51,380
Today, blimps are used primarily for advertising

743
00:40:51,480 --> 00:40:55,040
and can still draw the attention of everybody in sight.

744
00:40:55,140 --> 00:40:56,880
While their sophisticated mechanics

745
00:40:56,980 --> 00:41:00,880
make them much easier to steer than the zeppelins of the past,

746
00:41:00,980 --> 00:41:05,160
blimps are nowhere near the size of the giant airships.

747
00:41:05,260 --> 00:41:10,600
An average blimp has about 100,000 cubic feet of gas,

748
00:41:10,700 --> 00:41:13,140
helium nowadays.

749
00:41:13,240 --> 00:41:17,940
The Hindenburg had 7 and 1 half million cubic feet.

750
00:41:17,941 --> 00:41:20,340
Basically, a blimp today is about the length

751
00:41:20,440 --> 00:41:22,680
of the tail of the Hindenburg.

752
00:41:22,780 --> 00:41:24,940
A variety of blimps and tethered balloons

753
00:41:25,040 --> 00:41:29,080
are also used for communications platforms,

754
00:41:29,180 --> 00:41:33,720
observation, radar, drug interdiction, and border patrols.

755
00:41:33,820 --> 00:41:36,520
They are all filled with non-flammable helium

756
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:40,020
and seem to lack the romance, promise, and adventure

757
00:41:40,120 --> 00:41:43,940
of the great rigid airships.

758
00:41:44,040 --> 00:41:46,800
More than 60 years after the crash of the Hindenburg,

759
00:41:46,801 --> 00:41:51,240
the Zeppelin company is building a revolutionary new airship.

760
00:41:51,340 --> 00:41:52,800
It's called the NT07.

761
00:41:52,900 --> 00:41:54,940
It is a rigid airship in design,

762
00:41:55,040 --> 00:41:59,780
although it has very little structure inside.

763
00:41:59,880 --> 00:42:02,120
The new Zeppelin will seat 12

764
00:42:02,220 --> 00:42:05,120
and have a top speed of 90 miles an hour.

765
00:42:05,220 --> 00:42:07,220
Due to the airship's mechanical agility,

766
00:42:07,320 --> 00:42:10,720
its primary use will be for sightseeing.

767
00:42:10,820 --> 00:42:12,800
The advantage of the new Zeppelin

768
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:16,160
is incorporated in its tail thruster unit,

769
00:42:16,161 --> 00:42:19,860
which will allow the pilot to control the airship left, right,

770
00:42:19,960 --> 00:42:23,260
nose up and nose down at zero airspeed.

771
00:42:27,140 --> 00:42:30,100
This ability will reduce the number of ground crew needed

772
00:42:30,200 --> 00:42:32,040
on takeoff and landing.

773
00:42:32,140 --> 00:42:35,080
The ship's technical manager is a distant relative

774
00:42:35,180 --> 00:42:38,040
of Count von Zeppelin himself.

775
00:42:38,140 --> 00:42:43,220
Five Zeppelins have already been sold for $6.8 million each.

776
00:42:43,221 --> 00:42:47,400
The Germans will use the NT07 for tourism, advertising,

777
00:42:47,500 --> 00:42:49,300
and scientific research.

778
00:42:49,400 --> 00:42:53,460
The Swiss plan to use the new airship for sightseeing.

779
00:42:53,560 --> 00:42:57,160
Another German company plans to build an enormous rigid air

780
00:42:57,260 --> 00:43:00,980
ship to transport heavy cargo to faraway places.

781
00:43:01,080 --> 00:43:02,500
As far as I see from their plans,

782
00:43:02,600 --> 00:43:05,480
if they get away with this, their airship

783
00:43:05,580 --> 00:43:10,180
would be even much, much larger than the Hindenburg ever was.

784
00:43:10,280 --> 00:43:12,040
In fact, the planned cargo lifter

785
00:43:12,041 --> 00:43:16,440
is 3 and 1 half times the length of a 747

786
00:43:16,540 --> 00:43:19,580
and will have a payload of more than four times that

787
00:43:19,680 --> 00:43:24,420
of a jumbo jet or 175 tons.

788
00:43:24,520 --> 00:43:28,760
Estimated cost, $60 million.

789
00:43:28,860 --> 00:43:31,360
Located on a former Soviet military base,

790
00:43:31,460 --> 00:43:34,600
work has already begun on a hangar, which will house

791
00:43:34,700 --> 00:43:36,460
two cargo lifters.

792
00:43:36,560 --> 00:43:39,540
The huge airships will utilize solar energy

793
00:43:39,541 --> 00:43:42,940
and use one-tenth the fuel of a traditional 747.

794
00:43:44,140 --> 00:43:46,740
It seems within the foreseeable future, at least,

795
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:49,960
that we may indeed see the rebirth of the rigid airship.

796
00:43:50,340 --> 00:43:54,060
And that prospect holds the same promise as the original Zeppelin's.

797
00:43:54,440 --> 00:43:58,260
If you are lucky enough to ever see one, you will never forget it.

